// ── The body that makes the read-back loop iterate ──────────────
//
// 10000 bytes, which is 2 full HEXHOUND_OTA_CHUNK_MAX (4096) reads plus
// a short final one of 1808. Deliberately not a round multiple: the
// uneven remainder is the part of finish()'s loop most likely to be
// wrong, and a body that divided evenly would never show it.
//
// Every other body here is 1536 bytes and verifies in ONE pass, so
// this is the only fixture that exercises the arithmetic that lets a
// 1.2 MB image be checked on a board with 250 KB of RAM.
#define FIX_OTA_BIG_IMAGE_LEN 10000u

// The label the body is derived from. Same chained-SHA-512 rule as
// the literal bodies above:
//
//     block = SHA512(label); emit it; block = SHA512(block); repeat
//
// and truncate to FIX_OTA_BIG_IMAGE_LEN.
//
// ── Why this one is a rule and not 10000 literal bytes ──────────
//
// Because its value is its LENGTH and its position-dependence, not
// its literalness, and 10000 bytes of hex would add roughly 830
// lines to this file that no reader learns anything from.
//
// What keeps that honest: FIX_OTA_BIG_IMAGE_SHA512 below is the
// digest of the bytes the GENERATOR built, and it is also inside the
// signed FIX_OTA_HDR_BIG_OK. So the test's own filler has to agree
// with this generator byte for byte or nothing passes, and the
// agreement is checked directly rather than being assumed. A broken
// read-back loop cannot be papered over by adjusting the filler,
// because the filler's output has to hash to a digest that sits
// inside an Ed25519 signature.
//
// Assert against FIX_OTA_BIG_IMAGE_SHA512 FIRST in any test that
// uses this body. If the two fillers ever drift, that assertion says
// so plainly instead of surfacing as a baffling DIGEST_MISMATCH
// several layers down.
#define FIX_OTA_BIG_IMAGE_LABEL "hexhound-ota-fixture-image-big"

